    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. but the thing is, it feels less like \"good habits\" and more like a fundamental difference in how I'm wired. like, the amount of data i'm absorbing and the speed at which i'm connecting the dots... it's way beyond just being a \"morning person.\" it's almost like my brain is operating on a much higher bandwidth than the people around me. do you think there's any precedent for people having a naturally \"advanced\" or more optimized cognitive architecture compared to the standard human model?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While there isn't a single example of a person with a fundamentally optimized cognitive architecture, there are cases where individuals exhibit unusual cognitive abilities and adaptations. Research has identified phenomena such as Highly Superior Autobiographical Memory (HSAM), where some people retain an incredible number of personal memories, and Savant Syndrome, where exceptional individuals possess extraordinary cognitive abilities, like mathematical or artistic talents. Another theory is the concept of \" omega-3 enhanced cognition,\" which suggests that some people may have a more optimized brain-to-body mass ratio and better adaptation to stress, potentially leading to enhanced cognitive performance.",
      "source": "target",
